<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Fruit Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The United Fruit Company was an American corporation, founded in 1899, that dominated banana growing and trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Owning vast lands, railways and ports, it wielded great political influence, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, where dictators such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-ubico">Jorge Ubico</a> favoured it. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Jacobo Árbenz</a>'s land reform expropriated its unused land, it lobbied for the CIA-backed coup of 1954. It later became Chiquita Brands International.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company.jpg" alt="United Fruit Company" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Di (they-them) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_Fruit_Company_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
